hony sighed. [20] \"I have to take these chatty hens to the Rozz Graveyard.\" [21] \"That's the spirit,\" Jimmy said approvingly, \"They'll be all bruised and battered from the rough roads, and they'll be whining about needing to rest in bed. Then, we can go out for a couple of drinks and chat.\" [22] \"Great, see you later!\" [23] Anthony returned to the tour group, and the youngest and most frivolous Miss Taylor immediately made her move. [24] \"Mr. Kidd, is that your old friend?\" [25] \"Yes, Miss Taylor. An old friend from my youth.\" [26] Miss Taylor chuckled. [27] \"He's quite interesting-looking.\" [28] \"I'll pass that along to him.\" [29] \"Oh, Mr. Cade, you're so naughty! Always with the funny ideas. What did he call you?\" [30] \"Mr. Cox.\" [31] \"You're Cox?\" [32] \"Miss Taylor, I thought you knew I was Anthony.\" [33] \"Go away!\" Miss Taylor pouted and shrieked. [34] Now, Anthony was getting the hang of this job. In addition to arranging the necessary travel details, his responsibilities included: carefully consoling an irate old gentleman who had been offended, providing ample time for an elderly woman who wanted postcards, and flirting with women under forty. The last task was easier, as the women were always quick to pick up on the romantic undertones in his words. [35] Miss Taylor didn't let him off the hook. [36] \"Why does he call you 'Joe Gentleman'?\" [37] \"Because it's not my name.\" [38] \"Why does he call you 'Joe Gentleman'?\" [39] \"For the same reason.\" [40] \"Oh, Mr. Kidd, Miss Taylor protested, disheartened, \"don't speak like that. Dad said last night you had a gentleman's touch.\" [41] \"Thank you for your kind words, Miss Taylor.\" [42] \"We all think you're quite a gentleman.\" [43] \"I'm flattered.\" [44] \"I mean it.\" [45] \"A kind heart is more precious than a crown.\" Anthony mumbled incoherently, not knowing what he meant, wishing only to reach lunchtime. [46] \"I've always found this poem to be particularly beautiful. Do you know a lot of poetry, Mr. Kade?\" [47] \"I can recite a couple of lines: 'A boy stands on a burning deck; The rest are gone, save he alone.' That's all I know, but I can also do some actions with it: 'A boy stands on a burning deck' (breathes in) (breathes out) (gestures, showing fire) 'The rest are gone, save he alone,' and then I run around like a dog.\" [48] Miss Taylor laughed sharply. [49] \"Look at Mr. Kade! He's so funny!\" [50] \"It's time for tea,\" Anthony said, his tone lightening up, \"this way. There's a great caf\u00e9 just down the street.\" [51] \"So,\" Mrs. Gowan asks in a deep voice, \"is the tea included in the tour fee?\" [52] \"Mrs. Gowan,\" Anthony adopts a business-like tone, \"breakfast is self-paid.\" [53] \"What a shame!\" [54] \"Life is difficult,\" Anthony says with a placid expression. [55] Mrs. Gowan's eyes light up, and she exudes a sense of pride, \"I knew that was coming. So, I filled my water bottle with tea this morning. I'll just heat it up with an alcohol lamp later. Come on, old man!\" The Gowan couple strutted off towards the hotel, her back radiating a glow of self-satisfaction for her foresight. [56] \"What a mess!\" Anthony muttered under his breath, \"There are all kinds of birds in the woods!\" [57] He led the rest of the tourists to the caf\u00e9, with Miss Taylor walking beside him and continuing her questioning. [58] \"How long has it been since you and your friend last saw each other?\" [59] \"Seven years and a few months.\" [60] \"Did you meet in Africa?\" [61] \"Yes, but not in this area. The first time I saw Jimmy McGregor, he was tied up and about to be cooked. Some tribes in Africa eat people, you know that? We arrived just in time.\" [62] \"Then what happened?\" [63] \"We punished them by cooking a few of them, and the rest ran away.\" [64] \"Wow, Mr. Kidd. You live a life full of adventure!\" [65] \"Not really, just plain and simple.\" [66] Of course, in the eyes of that lady, his words were not believable. [