rue mastermind behind every political movement and resistance activity in the Near East. He is a dictator, a patriot, and a politician all in one. Everyone knows he is a conspirator, but no one knows what kind of person he really is. By the way, why did you bring him up?\" [105] \"Because he used to be the Prime Minister of Hesoslovakia.\" [106] \"It's not even comparable, Herscovitz is like a drop in the ocean compared to Count Stapleton. It's just his hometown and workplace. I thought he was dead, anyway.\" [107] \"He is dead. He died about two months ago in Paris. I'm talking about something that happened a few years ago.\" [108] \"What exactly are you going to tell me?\" [109] Jimmy quickly continued, \"Here's what happened. Four years ago, I was in Paris. One evening, I was walking through a quiet neighborhood when I saw five or six French thugs beating up an elderly gentleman who looked quite respectable. I hate seeing people being bullied, so I intervened and beat up the thugs badly.\" [110] \"Good job, James,\" Anthony's tone softened. \"I wish I could have seen that scene.\" [111] \"No big deal,\" James said modestly, \"The old gentleman was very grateful. He was probably drunk that day, but he still asked for my name and address, saying he would come to thank me seriously the next day. It was then that I realized that the old man I had saved was Count Stapleton, who was hiding near the Po River at the time.\" [112] Anthony nodded. [113] \"After the assassination of King Nikolai, Count Stapleton settled in Paris. Later, someone asked him to return to his country to become president. But he refused. He publicly supported constitutional monarchy, but it was rumored that he was involved in all the secret political actions on the Balkan Peninsula. Count Stapleton is too cunning.\" [114] \"So, Nikolai IV had a unique taste in choosing his wife, right?\" James suddenly changed the topic. [115] \"Yeah.\" Anthony said, \"He ruined himself with this, and it's a pity