tments, have her call and accept.\" [334] \"Yes, sir.\" [335] \"Oh, Everly, also call and check Mrs. Ruff's telephone number at 487 Pound Street for me.\" [336] \"Will do.\" Bill picked up the phone book, pretended to look down the list under the surname column, and then slammed the phone book shut and walked over to the desk. He put his hand on the phone receiver, as if suddenly remembering something, and hesitated for a moment. [337] \"Sir, I just remembered that Mrs. Rue's phone line is out. I called her a little while ago.\" [338] George Romax frowned. [339] \"Frustrating,\" he said. \"Very frustrating.\" He tapped the table lightly, hesitating. [340] \"If there's something important, I can go over there in a taxi right now. She should be home.\" [341] George Romax pondered for a moment, with Bill waiting anxiously by his side. If the answer was affirmative, he would rush out immediately. [342] \"Perhaps this is the best course of action,\" Romax finally said. \"You go in a taxi and ask Mrs. Ruff if she'll be home this afternoon at four o'clock. Tell her I have something very important to discuss with her.\" [343] \"Yes, sir.\" Bill grabbed his hat and quickly left. [344] Ten minutes later, a taxi dropped him off at 487 Pound Street. He rang the doorbell and knocked heavily on the door knocker. A serious-looking servant opened the door, and Bill nodded to her, recognizing her as an acquaintance. [345] \"Good morning, Chivers. Is Mrs. Ruff at home?\" [346] \"She is preparing to leave.\" [347] \"Bill, is that you?\" A voice came down the staircase, \"I knew it was you as soon as I heard the pounding on the door. Come up.\" [348] Bill looked up and saw a smiling face peering down at him, a face that always had a special charm that made anyone who saw it, including Bill, lose their composure. He bounded up the stairs two steps at a time, taking the hand that Virginia extended to him. [349] \"Hi, Virginia!\" [350] \"Hello, Bill!\" [351] Attraction is a mysterious thing: there are so many young women in the world, some even more beautiful than Virginia Ruef, and even if they say \"Hello, Bill\" with the same tone, it won't have any special effect. But when Virginia says those simple words, Bill feels spellbound. [352] Virginia was twenty-seven years old, tall and slender with a perfectly proportioned figure that was like poetry. Her hair was a pure copper, with a golden undertone and a hint of green. Her chin was small and determined, her nose cute, and her eyes blue. When she opened her eyelids, they glowed with a deep blue light, and her eyes sparkled. Her lips were always slightly upturned, giving her a wonderful, indescribable \"Venusian air\" that was full of expression. She exuded an aura of vitality, and wherever she went, she was the center of attention, and no one could ignore her charm. [353] She pulled Bill into the small living room, which was decorated in pale purple, green, and yellow, giving the impression of a field of daffodils hiding in the grass. [354] \"Bill, dear,\" Virginia said. \"I'm sure the State Department misses you. They can't function without you, can they?\" [355] \"I'm here to deliver a message from Mr. Kodes,\" Bill said disrespectfully, referring to his boss by his first name. [356] \"Oh, and if he asks, tell him your phone was broken this morning.\" [357] \"But it wasn't.\" [358] \"I know, but I told him it was.\" [359] \"Why? What's the story behind this diplomatic language?\" [360] Bill gives her a reproachful look. [361] \"Only then could I come and see you.\" [362] \"Oh, darling, you're so sweet!\" [363] \"Chivvers just said you were about to go out.\" [364] \"Yes, I'm going to Sloane Street. They've just brought out a lovely new girdle.\" [365] \"Girdle?\" [366] \"Yes, a garter\u2014belt\u2014a tight band worn around the hips.\" [367] \"What a shame! Talking about underwear with a young man like that. It's not very elegant.\" [368] \"But the hips aren't vulgar. Everyone has hips, but we poor women have to go to great lengths to pretend we don't. The garter belt is made of red rubber and goes up to just above the knee. It's a real pain to walk in.\" [369] \"That's terrible!\" said Bill. \"Why would you wear that?\" [370] \"Because women are willing to suffer for their figure. Let's not talk about garter belts anymore. What did George say?\" [371] \"He asked if you'll be home at 4 pm today.\" [372] \"No, I'm going to Larnaca. Why would he want to make such a formal visit? Is he going to propose to me?\" [373] \"If he does, I wouldn't be surprised.\" [374] \"If that's the case, tell him I prefer it when men propose out of the blue, spontaneously.\" [375] \"Like me?\" [376] \"You're not spontaneous, you're habitual.\" [377] \"Virginia, will you\u2026?\" [378] \"Alright, alright, Bill, don't be silly in the morning. You should treat me like a middle-aged woman with maternal instincts. I'll remember your feelings. \" [379] \"Virginia, I really love you.\" [380] \"I know, Bill, I know. I just like feeling loved. Am I bad for that? I hope all the good men in the world like me.\" [381] \"There are enough people who love you already.\" Bill said dejectedly. [382] \"But I don't want George to like me. I don't think he will either, he's married to his career. What else did he say?\" [383] \"He just said he had something important to discuss with you.\" [384] \"I'm getting more and more interested. There aren't many things that make George feel important. I'm not going to Rainerch today, I can go anytime. Tell George I'll be waiting for him at 4 o'clock.\" [385] Bill checked his wristwatch. [386] \"It's a pity to go back before lunch. Let's go out for some food, Virginia.\" [387] \"I'm going out for lunch.\" [388] \"It's okay, just stay with me today. Cancel all other arrangements.\" [389] \"Okay,\" Virginia smiled at him. [390] \"Virginia, you're so nice. You actually like me, don't you? You like me more than others, don't you?\" [391] \"Bill, I really like you.