e through his charm rather than his intellect, and he was well-suited for the position. His job required little responsibility and no brainpower, and he was like a little dog at George's side. His task was to be at George's beck and call, to run errands, and to make himself useful in any way he could. Bill did all of this diligently. When George was away, Bill would flop into the largest sofa chair and comfortably read the sports news, a tradition that had been going on for a long time. [410] George had grown accustomed to sending Bill on errands, and this time he sent Bill to the office of the Whitehall Shipping Company to inquire about the arrival time of the Granaanburgh. Like many well-bred British young men, Bill spoke with a pleasant voice but with some difficulty in pronunciation. The words \"Granaanburgh\" that he uttered would have made any speechmaster frown and sound awkward and out of place. The clerk at the shipping company heard \"Carnfrae.\" [411] The clerk said that Carnfrae would arrive on the following Thursday, and Bill thanked him before leaving. George Romilly received the message and adjusted his schedule accordingly. He had no knowledge of the Union Castle Line's sailing schedule and assumed that Jimmy McGlashan would arrive on Thursday. [412] Therefore, on Tuesday morning of that week, when he kept the Earl of Carringham waiting on the club steps, he was still unaware that the Granada had docked at Southampton the previous afternoon. At 2 pm that afternoon, Anthony Kede, a traveler with the name Jimmy Romilly, stepped out of the cabin and hailed a taxi at Waterloo Station. After a moment's thought, he instructed the driver to head to the Britz Hotel. [413] \"It's all right to relax now,\" Anthony said, gazing out the window with interest. [414] It had been fourteen years since he had last left London. [415] After checking into the hotel, he went for a walk along the riverbank. It was a joyous feeling to be back in London, although everything had changed. The Blackfriars Bridge area used to be a small restaurant where he and his friends often dined. He was a socialist back then, always wearing a red scarf. He was so young at the time. [416] He turned around and began to retrace his steps back to the hotel. Just as he was crossing the street, a man bumped into him full force, almost causing him to fall. Both men steadied themselves, and the man, who was of short stature with a muscular build, began to mutter an apology while staring intently at Anthony's face. He had the appearance of a typical working-class man, and seemed to be a foreigner. [417] Anthony was pondering the man's inquisitive gaze as he walked back. It was rather peculiar! Perhaps it was the man's tanned complexion that stood out among the pale Londoners. He returned to his room and, on a whim, walked up to the mirror to examine himself closely.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nBut I didn't think much of it.\" [713] \"We do have a waiter by that name,\" the manager says in a matter-of-fact tone. [714] \"Later, at night, the sound of someone walking softly in the room woke me up. I turned on the light and saw Gisippus rummaging through my suitcase.\" [715] The manager's indifference disappears from his face. [716] \"I had no idea about this,\" he exclaimed. \"Why wasn't I told earlier?\" [717] \"We struggled for a bit, and he had a knife. In the end, he ran out the window.\" [718] \"What did you do after that, Mr. McGlass?\" [719] \"I checked the contents of my suitcase.\" [720] \"Did anything go missing?\" [721] \"Just some trivial things,\" Anthony said slowly. [722] The manager sighed and leaned back in his chair. [723] \"That's good,\" he said. \"But if you don't mind, I'd like to know your thoughts on this. You didn't make a big fuss or chase after the thief?\" [724]