rtant detail. In these hotels, two of them had experienced major theft cases during Gis\u00e8ppe's tenure. Although he had no suspicious circumstances in both cases, the facts were clear. [741] Was Giuseppe just a skilled hotel thief? Did he steal Anthony's box as a professional trick? Or did he simply grab the envelope while Anthony was turning on the lights, and subconsciously put it in his pocket to free his hands? That would just be an ordinary theft. [742] But that couldn't explain Giuseppe's excitement when he saw the letter on the table the night before. There was no money or valuable items on the table that would arouse the interest of an ordinary thief. It wasn't like that. Anthony was convinced that Giuseppe was sent by someone. With the information provided by the manager, he could now get a glimpse into Giuseppe's private life, which might lead him to the man. [743] \"Thank you. I don't think we need to ask if Giuseppe is still in the hotel.\" [744] The manager smiled. [745] \"His bed hasn't been slept in, and his things are still here. He must have run away after the fight with you. We're unlikely to see him again.\" [746] \"Well, thank you. I'll have to stay here for a few more days.\" [747] \"I hope you find him, although I must admit the chances are slim.\" [748] \"I always think the best.\" [749] Anthony first asked several employees who were close to Giseppe, but he didn't get any leads. Then, he wrote an advertisement draft according to his plan and sent it to five newspapers with the largest circulation. Just as he was about to leave to visit the restaurant where Giseppe used to work, the phone rang. Anthony picked up the receiver: \"Hello, who is this?\" [750] A hoarse voice came from the other end of the line. [751] \"I'm looking for Mr. McGrath.\" [752] \"This is McGrath. Who is this?\" [753] \"This is Puddington Haggis Publishing. Please hold on, I'll transfer you to Mr. Puddington.\" [754] \"That big publishing house,\" Anthony thought. \"Are they in a hurry? There's still a week left.\" [755] Then he heard a cheerful voice. [756] \"Hello! Is this Mr. McGlass?\" [757] \"Yes.\" [758] \"This is Mr. Pottsford from Pottsford Haggard Publishing. How's the manuscript doing?\" [759] \"Uh, \" Anthony said. \"Which part are you referring to?\" [760] \"All of it, Mr. McGlass. You just arrived from South Africa and are not very familiar with the situation here. The manuscript has a lot of problems, big problems. Sometimes I think, if only I hadn't agreed to this.\" [761] \"Really?\" [762] \"Absolutely. Now I'm just thinking about quickly getting the manuscript copied a few times, so that even if the original is destroyed, it won't be a big loss.\" [763] \"My goodness!\" said Anthony. [764] \"I know it sounds ridiculous to you. But you really don't understand the current situation. There are people trying to prevent us from getting the manuscript, and they don't want us to have it. And I'm being honest with you, if you want to bring the manuscript yourself, you probably won't make it here.\" [765] \"I don't believe it.\" said Anthony. \"I can do whatever I want to do.\" [766] \"You're facing a very dangerous group of people. A month ago, I didn't believe it either. I'm telling you, Mr. McGregor, people have been bribing, threatening, and manipulating us to get that manuscript. I suggest you don't bring the manuscript yourself. I'll send someone to your hotel to pick it up.\" [767] \"What if that person gets taken care of?\" Anthony asked. [768] \"That's on us, not on you. You just give it to our person, and they'll give you a written statement. We agreed with the late... uh... author, you know who, that we should pay you \u00a31,000. It will be sent to you as a check next Wednesday. But if you insist on receiving the money first, I can write you a check for the same amount, and have that person bring it to you.\" [769] Anthony thought for a moment. He had planned to wait until the last minute to bring the manuscript, as he wanted to see what was so shocking about it. But he realized the publisher had a valid point. [770] \"Alright.\" He sighed softly. \"Do as you say. Send someone over. And bring the check, as I might leave Britain before next Wednesday.\" [771] \"Certainly, Mr. McGrath. Our person will come to you first thing tomorrow morning. I think it would be best if he doesn't come from the office. Mr. Holmes of our company lives in the south of London, and he can stop by your place on his way to work. He will bring you a receipt. I suggest you put a fake manuscript in the hotel manager's safe tonight to confuse things. If your enemies get wind of this, they won't raid your room tonight.\" [772] \"Okay, I'll do that,\" Anthony said thoughtfully, hanging up the phone. [773] He then resumed his interrupted plan to find out more about the cunning Giuseppe. But he found nothing. Giuseppe had worked in that restaurant, but no one knew anything about his personal life or social circle. [774] \"I'll find you, you little son of a gun,\" Anthony growled to himself. \"I'll find you, sooner or later.\" [775] The second night in London was very quiet. At 9 a.m. the next morning, Mr. Holmes of Packerton Haggard Publishers was led upstairs. He was short, fair-haired, and not very talkative. Anthony handed him the manuscript, and received a check for \u00a31,000 in return. Mr. Holmes put the manuscript in his brown bag, wished Anthony good morning, and left. Everything went smoothly. [776] \"But he might have been killed on the way,\" Anthony said to himself, staring out the window, \"I'm so curious, so curious.\" [777] He put the check in an envelope, wrote a few lines on it, and sealed it carefully. Jimmy had given Anthony a large sum of money when they met in Blavet, so the money was still untouched. [778] \"One thing down, another to go,\" Anthony thought to himself, \"I messed up on this one, but never say die! I can change my identity and check out 487 Pound Street.\" [779] He packed his bags, checked out of the hotel, and instructed the bellboy to put his luggage in a taxi. Although the hotel staff hadn't done much to make him feel comfortable, he still tipped them. As the taxi was about to depart, a boy ran down the stairs and handed him a letter. [780] \"Sir, this just arrived for you.\" Anthony sighed and gave him another penny. The taxi roared, leaped forward, and moved off with a loud clatter of gears. Anthony opened the letter. [781] It was a strange document that he read four times to understand. Essentially (the letter was not written in simple terms but in the unique style of government correspondence), they believed that Mr. McGlashan arrived in Britain from South Africa on Thursday. It vaguely mentioned the memoirs of the Earl of Stapleton and requested that he not act until he had spoken in confidence with Mr. George Romax and others, all of whom were implied to be very important figures. The letter explicitly invited him to visit the Chimney House as the guest of the Earl of Chatham the following day, Friday. [782] It was a mysterious and cryptic way of expressing himself, but Anthony liked it. [783] \"Old England,\" he murmured with affection, \"always half a step behind the times, it's a shame. Although I've been looking for a convenient hotel, I can't just waltz into Chimney House under the name of Anthony Kede.\" [784] He leaned out the window and indicated a few new directions to the driver, who snorted in disdain. [785] The taxi stopped at a secluded inn, but the driver charged him as if he were a hotel guest. [786] He booked a room under the name of Anthony Kidd and then went into a dimly lit office. He took out a sheet of paper with the letterhead of the Blitz Hotel and hastily wrote a letter. [787] He explained that he had arrived on Tuesday of the previous week and had already handed over the manuscript mentioned in the letter to the Puddleby-Haggick Publishing House. He regretfully declined the Earl of Carringham's invitation, as he was about to leave Britain. Finally, he signed the letter \"Jimmy McGregor.\" [788] \"Now,\" Anthony said to himself as he stuck on the stamp, \"it's time to get down to business. Jimmy McGregor exits, Anthony Kidd takes the stage.\" [789] Chapter Eight: A Dead Man\n[790] On the same Thursday afternoon, Virginia Rue was playing tennis in Ranelagh. On her way back to Pound Street, she leaned back in the limousine and rehearsed her role in the upcoming conversation. Of course, the blackmailer might not appear again, but she felt he would. In their previous encounters, she had deliberately portrayed herself as a simple-minded fool. This time, she intended to catch him off guard. [791] The car pulled up to the house, and she chatted with the driver before climbing the steps. [792] \"Walton, I almost forgot to ask, how's your wife doing?\" [793] \"She's much better, ma'am. The doctor will check on her around six-thirty. Will you need the car again?\" [794] Virginia considered for a moment. [795] \"I'm leaving for the weekend. I have to leave Paddington at six forty. But you don't need to come, I'll just call a taxi. It would be better for you to see the doctor. If the doctor thinks it's good for your wife to get out and about this weekend, you can take her out. The expenses are on me.\" [796] The driver thanked her profusely, and Virginia impatiently nodded and ran up the stairs. She rummaged through her bag for a while, realizing she had forgotten her key when she left, and quickly pressed the doorbell. [797] The person inside did not answer immediately, and while she waited, a young man walked up the stairs. He was dressed modestly and held a bundle of brochures. He handed her one, which clearly read \"Why Should I Serve My Country?\" in bold letters, and he also held a collection box in his left hand. [798] \"Such a poor collection of poems, I can't buy two copies in a day, can I?\" Virginia pleaded. \"I bought one this morning, really, I swear on my honor.\" [799] The young man threw his head back and laughed heartily; Virginia joined in. She glanced at him and noticed that he was more pleasant to look at than the usual unemployed people she saw. His dark complexion and rugged features appealed to her, and she even considered offering him a job. [800] Just then, the door opened, and Virginia quickly forgot about the unemployed man, as she was surprised to find her maid, Alice, standing there. [801] \"Where's Chifford?\" she asked sternly as she walked into the hallway. [802] \"He went with the others, ma'am,\" Alice replied. [803] \"Who are the others? Where did they go?\" [804] \"To Datchet, the villa, ma'am, as you mentioned in your telegram.\" [805] \"My telegram?\" Virginia was completely bewildered. [806] \"You didn't send a telegram? No, it was just an hour ago.\" [807] \"I never sent a telegram. What did it say?\" [808] \"It should still be on the table.\" [809] Alice went off to fetch the telegram, and triumphantly handed it to Virginia. [810] \"Here you are, ma'am.\" [811] The telegram was addressed to Chiffers and read: \"Come with the servants to the villa for the weekend gathering, take the 5:49 train.\" [812] The telegram seemed completely ordinary, just like the one Alice had sent out spontaneously to host a gathering at the Riverside Villa. She often took the entire staff with her, leaving only an old maid behind to watch the house. Chiffers thought nothing of it and simply carried out his duties as a loyal servant. [813] \"I stayed behind,\" Alice explained. \"I knew ma'am needed me to pack her bags.\" [814] \"This is a ridiculous prank,\" Virginia exclaimed, angrily throwing the telegram to the ground. \"Alice, you know I'm going to the Chimney Villa, I told you this morning.\" [815] \"I thought ma'am changed her mind,\" Alice replied. \"Isn't that what usually happens?\" [816] Virginia had no response and reluctantly gave up. All she wanted to know was why someone would pull such an unusual prank. Alice proposed a theory. [817] \"My goodness!\" she exclaimed, clasping her hands together. \"What if it's a bad person? They sent a fake telegram, got rid of the servants, and then they could rob us.\" [818] \"I think it's very likely,\" Virginia said suspiciously. [819] \"Yes, yes, ma'am, it must be. There are reports like that in the newspapers every day. You must call the police right away, or they'll come and cut our throats when it's too late.\" [820] \"Don't be so dramatic, they won't come and cut our throats at six o'clock in the afternoon.\" [821] \"Ma'am, please let me go out and call the police right now.\" [822] \"Why? Don't be silly, Alice. If you haven't packed the luggage for the smokehouse villa yet, go ahead and prepare it. Pack the new Giallo evening gown, the white silk gown, and the black silk gown. The black silk gown would be perfect for a political party, wouldn't it?\" [823] \"Ma'am, the green evening gown would be the most charming!\" [824] \"No, I'm not taking that one. Hurry up, Alice. We don't have much time. I'll send a telegram to Chiffers in Datchet. When we leave, also tell the patrolling police to keep an eye on this place. Stop rolling your eyes, Alice. You're already scared before anything happens, and what will you do when someone suddenly jumps out from the corner with a knife?\" [825] Alice let out a piercing scream and quickly ran upstairs, glancing nervously around as she went. [826] Virginia made a face at her back and walked through the hall to the small study. The phone was in the study, and Alice's suggestion to call the police seemed like a good idea, so she decided to do it without delay. [827] She opened the study door and walked over to the phone. As she put her hand on the receiver, she stopped. She had forgotten all about the waiting guest, so engrossed had she been in her own worries. He must have fallen asleep while waiting for her. [828] She smiled mischievously as she walked over to the chair. But her smile disappeared. [829] The man was not asleep, but dead. [830] There was a small hole, the size of a fingernail, in the man's heart, and blood was seeping out in a dark red color; his chin was hanging down, and he looked terrifying; a gleaming small gun lay on the floor. But Virginia had already sensed what had happened instinctively before she saw all this. [831] She stood there blankly, her hands pressed tightly against her sides. In the silence, she heard Alice's footsteps down the stairs. [832] \"Ma'am, ma'am.\" [833] \"What? What's wrong?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[3140] \"Do you think someone is trying to play a trick on us?\u201d his father said nervously, \"I don't trust that Frenchman, L\u00e9on. The French police can pull off all sorts of tricks. They'll tie rubber bands around your arm, tell you the crime story, make you feel terrible, record your temperature with a thermometer, and then yell, 'Who killed the Prince of Michael?' My temperature might be 122 degrees or even more terrifying. Then they'll drag me straight to jail.\" [3141] The door opened, and Tredwell announced:\n[3142] \"Mr. George Romax, Mr. Everleigh.\" [3143] \"Cordes arrived, bringing his faithful hound,\" Bunder murmured. [3144] Bill walked directly to Bunder's side, while George greeted the Earl of Carringham with warmth, putting on his usual show in public. [3145] \"Dear Carringham,\" George said, shaking his hand, \"I came as soon as I received your message.\" [3146] \"You're too good, really. It's good to see you.\" [3147] The Duke of Carrington, always afraid of being impolite, unconsciously overdid his courtesy: \"It wasn't my message, but it doesn't matter.\" [3148] Meanwhile, Bill whispered to Bandel. [3149] \"What's going on? I heard Virginia ran off in the middle of the night. Did she get kidnapped?\" [3150] \"No,\" Bandel said, \"she left a note, pinned to the cushion, in her style.\" [3151] \"Did she go alone? Not with that American colonial John? I never liked him, and there are rumors that he's a con artist. But it doesn't seem like it.\" [3152] \"Why doesn't it seem like it?\" [3153] \"Victor is a Frenchman, and Kade is a pure Englishman.\" [3154] \"You haven't heard that Victor is a linguistic genius? And he has half Irish blood.\" [3155] \"My goodness! That's why he never shows up anymore?\" [3156] \"I don't know if he'll show up or not, but he was gone yesterday. But this morning he sent a telegram saying he'll come tonight at nine o'clock and have us invite Kidd over. Other people also came, all invited by Kidd.\" [3157] \"Quite a gathering,\" Bill said, looking around. \"The French detective sitting by the window, the British detective by the fireplace. Such a rich mix of nationalities. Where's the representative of the Stars and Stripes?\" [3158] Bender shook his head. [3159] \"Mr. Fischer has disappeared without a trace. Virginia is also missing. But everyone else is here. Bill, I have a feeling that the moment of truth is about to arrive, and soon someone will shout, 'It's James, the butler!' We're just waiting for Kidd to arrive.\" [3160] \"He won't show up,\" Bill said. [3161] \"Then why call this a 'company meeting'? That's what my father named it.\" [3162] \"There's definitely more to it. He obviously wants us to gather here, and he's probably somewhere else, you know the kind of trick he likes to pull.\" [3163] \"You mean he won't come?\" [3164] \"Of course not. Would he still walk into a lion's den? This meeting hall is filled with detectives and high-ranking officials.\" [3165] \"If you think so, then you don't know Victor King well. It's said that this is his favorite scene, and he always manages to escape unscathed.\" [3166] Eversley shakes his head skeptically. [3167] \"This is against all odds. He won't\u2026\"\n[3168] The door opens, and Tredwell announces:\n[3169] \"Mr. Cade.\" [3170] Anthony strides towards the host. [3171] \"Lord Carterham.\" He says, \"I do apologize for the inconvenience, but I believe that tonight, all secrets will be revealed.\" [3172] Lord Carterham seems relieved, and he has always had an inexplicable fondness for Anthony. [3173] \"No trouble at all.\" He says sincerely. [3174] \"You are too kind.\" Anthony says, \"Everyone is here, so I will begin.\" [3175] \"I don't understand,\" George Romax pretends to be confused, \"I don't understand. This is irregular. What is Mr. Kidd's status? The situation is delicate and complicated. I strongly suggest\u2026\"\n[3176] George's eloquent speech was interrupted by the Sheriff quietly walking over to him and whispering something. After a moment, George suddenly looked puzzled. [3177] \"Well, if you say so.\" He said reluctantly. Then, he loudly added, \"Let's hear what he has to say.\" [3178] Anthony did not let the obvious contempt bother him. [3179] \"It's just a small idea, that's all.\" He said cheerfully. \"As you all know, we received a cipher a few days ago that mentioned Richmond and some numbers. We attempted to decrypt it, but failed. Now, using the late Count Stapleton's memoirs\u2014which I happened to read\u2014we can solve this puzzle. The memoirs mentioned a dinner party with a 'flower' theme, where all the guests wore floral symbols. The Count's own badge, which we found in the strange emblem on the wall of the secret passage, represents a rose. You should remember the rows of things: buttons, the letter E, and weaving. Now, what in this villa has rows? Books, right? And in the Earl of Carrington's library catalog, there is a book titled 'The Earl of Richmond.' Now you all know where the hidden jewels are. From that book, using those numbers to search the bookshelves and book numbers, our long-sought item should be hidden in a fake book or a cave behind a book.\" [3180] Anthony modestly looked around, clearly waiting for applause from the audience. [3181] \"That's too clever!\" said the Earl of Carringham. [3182] \"Very clever,\" George modestly admitted. \"But the facts still need to be confirmed.\" [3183] Anthony laughed loudly. [3184] \"Practice makes perfect, right? I'll show you.\" He jumped up. \"I'll go to the study\u2026\"\n[3185] Before he could take another step, Mr. Lemon walked over from the window. [3186] \"Wait a moment, Mr. Kidd. Marquis of Carringham, if I may?\" [3187] He walked over to the desk, hastily scribbled a few lines, put them in an envelope, and rang the bell. [3188] Tredwell rushed in. [3189] \"Please deliver this immediately,\" said Lemon. [3190] \"Yes, sir,\" said Tredwell. [3191] He withdrew with his usual dignity. [3192] Anthony, who had been standing there hesitantly, sat down again. [3193] \"What do you think, Lermontov?\" he asked mildly. [3194] Suddenly, the air was filled with a tense atmosphere. [3195] \"If the diamond is where you say it is, it has been hidden for over seven years, so it doesn't matter if it's hidden for a little longer.\" [3196] \"Go on,\" said Anthony. \"You're not finished yet.\" [3197] \"Yes, I'm not finished. It would be unwise to let anyone leave the room at this point, especially the person who is unknown to us.\" [3198] Anthony raised his eyebrows and lit a cigarette. [3199] \"I think a life of wandering is not very respectable,\" he pondered. [3200] \"Mr. Kidd, two months ago, you were in South Africa. That is undeniable. And before that, where were you?\" [3201] Anthony leaned back in his chair, puffing on his cigarette rings. [3202] \"Canada, the wilds of the northwest.\" [3203] \"Are you sure you haven't been in jail? French jail?\" [3204] Detective Baudet took a step towards the door, as if to block the exit. But Anthony showed no sign of retaliation. [3205] Instead, he looked at the French detective and burst out laughing. [3206] \"Poor Lemont, you're obsessed! You see everyone as the Duke of Vicor. So, you