 the description in the documents\u2026\"\n[3221] \"Yes, if everything matches,\" he asked softly. [3222] Anthony leaned forward in his chair. [3223] \"Yes, if everything matches,\" he asked softly, \"so what?\" [3224] \"So what?\" the detective seemed surprised. \"Then I'll prove that you're Captain Victor King!\" [3225] But for the first time, a flicker of uncertainty crossed his face. [3226] \"Certainly, you would be satisfied,\" Anthony said, \"But I don't see how it would hurt me? I'm not admitting I am Victor King. But for the sake of argument, if I were Victor King, I might be trying to turn over a new leaf.\" [3227] \"Turn over a new leaf?\" [3228] \"Just an assumption. If you were Victor King, use your imagination. You just got out of prison, ready to start a new life, no longer with the thrill of adventure; you even met a beautiful girl and planned to settle down, marry, and retire. Live a quiet life from then on. Could you relate to that feeling?\" [3229] \"I wouldn't think so.\" Lemont's face showed a mocking smile. [3230] \"Maybe not,\" Anthony admitted, \"but you're not him?\" [3231] \"Your words are nonsense.\" [3232] \"Not at all nonsense, Lemont. Tell me, if I were the Vicomte, what crime could you accuse me of? The evidence of those old crimes is long gone. I've served my time, and it's wiped clean. You could try to find a French law based on 'loitering with intent to commit a crime,' but you'd still be unsatisfied, wouldn't you?\" [3233] \"You forgot,\" Lemont said. \"America! You impersonated the Prince Nicolas Paulvich, that's a crime.\" [3234] \"Useless, Lemont,\" Anthony said. \"At that time, I had nothing to do with America, and I could prove it without much effort. If the Vicomte had impersonated Prince Nicolas in America, then I wouldn't be the Vicomte. Are you sure it was an impersonation, and not the Prince Nicolas himself?\" [3235] Suddenly, Chief Constable Bettlebutt interjected, \"Mr. Kidd, that man is a fraud.\" [3236] \"Sheriff Batter, I won't argue with you,\" Anthony said. \"You're always right. So, you believe Prince Nicolas died in Congo?\" [3237] Sheriff Batter looked at him curiously. [3238] \"I'm not sure, but everyone says so.\" [3239] \"Careful with your words,\" Anthony said. \"What was your motto? 'Do not do evil, lest evil befall you.' I took a leaf out of your book and let Lemont say whatever he wanted, without denying it. But I'm afraid he'll be disappointed. You know I always have a trump card. I've brought one here, to be precise, a person, upstairs.\" [3240] \"Upstairs?\" said the Marquis de Carterham with interest. [3241] \"Yes, the poor fellow's been having a rough time lately, his head's been beaten pretty badly. I've been taking care of him.\" [3242] Suddenly, Mr. Issakov's deep voice came from the side: \"Can we guess who it is?\" [3243] \"Be my guest.\" Anthony said, \"But -\"\n[3244] Mr. Lemont interrupted him angrily:\n[3245] \"All nonsense! You think you're smarter than me. What you say might be true, you weren't in America at the time. If it weren't true, you wouldn't have said it. But there's another thing, a murder! Yes, a murder! Prince Michael's murder. That night when you were looking for the diamond, he ruined your scheme.\" [3246] \"Lemaire, have you heard of Victor King killing anyone?\" Anthony's voice was sharp. \"You know better than me, he never kills.\" [3247] \"Who else would kill him?\" Lemaire shouted. \"Tell me!\" [3248] As he finished speaking, a sharp whistle came from the corridor. Anthony put aside his previous indifference and jumped up. [3249] \"Didn't you ask me who killed Michael Prince?\" he called. \"I don't need to tell you, I'll show you. That whistle is the signal I've been waiting for. The murderer of Michael is now in the library.\" [3250] He jumped out of the window, followed by the others, circling the platform, and coming to the window of the library. He pushed it, and the window opened. [3251] He gently pulled the thick window curtain aside, and everyone could see the scene inside the room. [3252] A shadow stood in front of the bookshelf, intently pulling out one book after another and putting them back, completely oblivious to the noise outside. [3253] The figure held a flashlight, the dim light of which vaguely outlined his silhouette, and everyone stood there trying to recognize his appearance. Suddenly, someone jumped past them, letting out a roar like a wild beast. [3254] The flashlight fell to the ground and went out, and the room was filled with the sounds of terrifying struggle. The Earl of Carrington felt his way to the switch and turned on the light. [3255] Two people were wrestling, and when the crowd surrounded them, everything was over. A quick gunshot, and the short man bent over and fell to the ground. The other turned around to face everyone, it was Percy. His eyes were filled with anger. [3256] \"She killed my master,\" he roared, \"and now she wants to kill me. I took the gun from her and pointed it at her, but it went off during the struggle. It was the orders of the Duke of Michael, and this evil woman is dead.\" [3257] \"A woman?\" George Romax exclaimed. [3258] They approached the body, which lay on the floor, holding the gun in her hand, with a sinister expression on her face. It was Miss White. [3259] * * *\n[3260] [1] Alphonse Bertillon: A French Parisian clerk in the Identification Department in the 19th century; he developed the first modern system of criminal identification. This system, known as \"Bertillonage,\" consists of three parts: physical measurements, accurate descriptions of the physical characteristics of the suspect, and standardized facial photographs. [3261] Chapter 28: The Prince Victor\n[3262] \"I suspected her from the beginning,\" Anthony said, \"There was light in her room on the night of the murder. Later, I was unsure. I went to Bridesdale to investigate her, and when I returned, I believed she was Miss White. I was stupid at the time. Because 