 mystery to me. If I had known your true identity earlier, I would have arrested you based on the evidence.\" [3345] \"I'm glad I kept that secret under wraps. You've dug up everything else. Constable Batter, you're a real pro in your field. I will remain in awe of Scotland Yard.\" [3346] \"It's incredible,\" George murmured. \"I've never heard of anything so incredible. Baron, do you really believe...\"\n[3347] \"Dear Romax,\" Anthony said sternly. \"I do not intend to request support from the British Foreign Office until I have presented a compelling written argument. I suggest we adjourn now, and I will discuss the loan agreement conditions with the Baron and Mr. Issakov.\" [3348] The Baron stood up, bringing his feet together. [3349] \"To meet the King of Hesse, it is the most glorious moment of my life,\" he said solemnly. [3350] \"By the way, Baron,\" Anthony said, hooking his arm around the other man's, \"I forgot to tell you, there's an important piece of information. I'm already married.\" [3351] The Baron took a step back, his face full of disappointment. [3352] \"I knew there would be trouble,\" he said under his breath, \"Oh my God! He married a black man in Africa!\" [3353] \"Well, it's not as bad as that,\" Anthony laughed. \"She's quite white, from head to toe.\" [3354] \"Well, that's a decent match between a noble and a commoner.\" [3355] \"No, that's not it. She's going to be my queen. Don't shake your head, she's more than capable. She's a British noblewoman, her family can be traced back to King William I. Royal marriages between nobility are quite popular these days, and she knows a thing or two about Heslovakia.\" [3356]\"Oh, my!\" George Roxburgh exclaimed, breaking his usual eloquent speech. \"Could it be... could it be... Virginia Rufe?\" [3357]\"Yes,\" Anthony said. \"Virginia Rufe.\" [3358]\"Goodness, sir!\" the Earl of Carmarthen exclaimed. \"I mean... congratulations! She's a beauty.\" [3359]\"Thank you,\" Anthony said. \"She's more than that.\" [3360]However, Mr. Essex was curiously examining him. [3361] \"Excuse me, sir, when did you get married?\" [3362] Anthony smiled at him. [3363] \"Actually,\" he said, \"we got married this morning.\" [3364] Chapter 30: Anthony Takes Up New Position\n[3365] \"You all go ahead, I'll be along shortly.\" said Anthony. [3366] The others filed out of the room, and Anthony saw Chief Batterton staring at the wall paneling, so he walked over to him. [3367] \"Chief Batterton, you have something to ask me, don't you?\" [3368] \"Yes, sir. Although I don't know how you knew, you have a knack for understanding things that others don't. I believe the deceased lady was Queen Faraja, wasn't she?\" [3369] \"Correct. I hope this stays between us, you understand, family matters should not be discussed publicly.\" [3370] \"Mr. Roxes is very discreet and careful, and no one will know. My point is, many people will know, but it won't be spread outside.\" [3371] \"Is that what you wanted to ask me?\" [3372] \"No, sir. I just mentioned it casually. I'm curious, why did you choose to give up your real name? Is it too forward to ask?\" [3373] \"Not at all, I'll tell you everything. The reason I killed myself was actually very simple. My mother was British, and I was educated in Britain, so I cared more about Britain than Hessoslovakia. I thought it was ridiculous to roam the world with a ridiculous opera-style title. When I was young, I advocated for democratic ideas, and I believed that ideals must be pure and everyone should be equal. I especially opposed the monarchy.\" [3374] \"What happened next?\" [3375] \"After that, I traveled the world and found that there was hardly any equality. However, I still believe in democracy. But to implement democracy, it must be forcibly imposed in a dictatorial manner. People won't become brothers, but someday they will. But now it's not possible. Last week, when I arrived in London, I saw that the people standing in the subway refused to move a few steps to make room for those just getting on. At that moment, my belief in the brotherhood of all under heaven was shattered. It's unrealistic to turn them into angels by nature, so we can only force them to coexist peacefully in a wise way. I still believe in the idea of the brotherhood of all under heaven, but it's not the time yet. For example, in another 10,000 years. We need patience, evolution is a slow process.\" [3376] \"Your ideas fascinate me, sir.\" Bardell's eyes sparkled. \"You would make a wise king, and I hope I'm not offending you by saying so.\" [3377] \"Thank you, Chief Bardell,\" Anthony sighed. [3378] \"You don't seem happy.\" [3379] \"I don't know, maybe it will be interesting. But fixed work is always boring, I always avoid it.\" [3380] \"But you think it's your duty, right?\" [3381] \"Oh, no! Too fanciful. It's for a woman, I can't get away from women. I would do anything for her, even become a king.\" [3382] \"That's good.\" [3383] \"I've arranged everything so that both the baron and Azerbaijan won't object. They both need a king and oil, and I... Chief Inspector Battel, have you ever felt love?\" [3384] \"I love my wife very much.\" [3385] \"Love your wife... Oh, you didn't understand what I meant. I'm not talking about that.\" [3386] \"Sorry. Your servant is waiting for you outside the window.\" [3387] \"Poussin? Ah, it is him. He's a remarkable man, fortunately, the gun went off during the fight and killed the lady. Otherwise, he would have strangled her. Then, he would have been hanged by you. He is very loyal to the House of Paulovitch, and it's strange that he followed me when Michael died, not knowing my true identity at the time.\" [3388] \"Instinct,\" said Bartel. \"Dog-like instinct.\" [3389] \"His instinct was very embarrassing to me at the time, and I was afraid I would give myself away in front of you. I'll go see what he's up to.\" [3390] He walked out through the French doors, and Bartel the chief of police stood there for a moment, watching his back. Then, he said to the panel on the wall, \"He'll make a good king.\" [3391] Outside the window, Berceuse was explaining his purpose. [3392] \"Master,\" he said, and led the way down the corridor. [3393] Anthony followed behind him, not knowing what lay ahead. [3394] After a while, Berceuse stopped and pointed forward. [3395] In the moonlight, two people were sitting on the stone bench in front. [3396] \"He's still a dog,\" Anthony thinks to himself, \"and a pointer!\" [3397] He strides over, while Boris disappears into the darkness.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nWould any of his old friends recognize him if they saw him now? He slowly shook his head. [418] When he left London, he was only eighteen, a fair-skinned, round-faced boy with an expression that seemed innocent. Now, looking at himself in the mirror, he was gaunt, tanned, and had an expression that was bizarre. He had completely transformed, and no one would recognize him. [419] The phone by the bed rang, and Anthony walked over to pick up the receiver. [420] \"Hello!\" [421] It was the voice of the night clerk. [422] \"Is this Mr. Jimmy McGregor?\" [423] \"Yes, who is it?\" [424] \"A gentleman wants to see you.\" [425] Anthony was taken aback. [426] \"See me?\" [427] \"Yes, sir, a foreigner.\" [428] \"Who is he?\" [429] The waiter paused for a moment and then replied, \"I'll have his business card sent to you.\" [430] Anthony put down the phone and waited a few minutes, then there was a knock on the door. A young waiter brought a tray with the business card. [431] Anthony took the business card and saw that it read \"Baron Lothair Rottschl\u00e4ger.\" [432] He suddenly understood why the waiter had hesitated earlier. [433] He thought for a moment and made up his mind. [434] \"Let this gentleman come up.\" [435] \"Certainly, sir.\" [436] A few minutes later, Baron Lollopriate Yurchell was led into the room. He was tall and had a thick, fan-shaped beard, with a balding forehead that stood out. [437] He snapped his heels together and bowed deeply to Anthony. \"Mr. McGrath.\" [438] Anthony mimicked the gesture. \"Baron.\u201d Then he pulled out a chair. \"Please, sit. I believe I haven't had the pleasure of meeting you before?\" [439] \"We haven't met.\" The baron sat down politely. \"That's my misfortune.\" [440] \"That's mine as well.\" Anthony replied in the same tone. [441] \"I am here on serious business.\" The baron said. \"I have come today as a representative of the Hesslovian royal court in London.\" [442] \"You are an exemplary representative indeed.\" Anthony whispered. [443] The baron slightly bowed in appreciation for the compliment. [444] \"I am unworthy of such praise.\" The baron said stiffly. \"Mr. McGrath, I must confess. Our restoration efforts have been on hold since the demise of the gracious Nicholas IV. Now the time has come.\" [445] \"Amen.\" Anthony murmured. \"Please continue.\" [446] \"We support Prince Michael's ascension to the throne, and the British government supports him as well.\" [447] \"Excellent,\" Anthony said. \"Thank you for sharing this with me.\" [448] \"Everything is in place, but your presence brings trouble,\" the baron said, glaring at him. [449] \"Baron, I protest,\" Anthony said. [450] \"I am not joking, your presence brings back memories of the late Count Stapleton's memoirs,\" he said, staring intently into Anthony's eyes. [451] \"So what?\" Anthony asked, \"What does the Earl of Stapleton's memoirs have to do with Prince Michael?\" [452] \"It will cause gossip.\" [453] \"Any memoir will do that.\" Anthony tried to comfort him. [454] \"He knows many secrets. If he reveals even a fraction, Europe could descend into chaos.\" [455] \"Don't think that way,\" Anthony said, \"How could that be possible?\" [456] \"The gossip about the Paulvich family will spread outside, and the British are very democratic.\" [457] \"I know the Paulvich family is a bit tyrannical,\" Anthony said. \"It's in their blood. But in the British mindset, the Balkan states should be like that, although the reason is unclear, they do think that way.\" [458] \"You don't understand,\" the baron said. \"You don't know, and I can't tell you.\" [459] \"What exactly are you afraid of?\" Anthony asked. [460] \"I'll find out when I read the memoirs,\" the baron said simply. \"But there will be something. Those diplomats don't know how to keep their mouths shut. It's a shame.\" [461] \"Don't be like that,\" Anthony said mildly. \"I think you're too pessimistic. I know enough about publishers to know that they incubate books like eggs, at least they have to wait a year or so.\" [462] \"You're either too cunning or too naive, young man,\" the baron said. \"A weekly newspaper's office is all set up, and as soon as the memoirs arrive, they'll be published.\" [463] \"Ah!\" Anthony was surprised. \"But you can deny it.\" [464] The baron shook his head sadly. [465] \"It's not like that, not like that, you're just making it up. Let's talk about our business. You'll get a thousand pounds, right? I'm so well-informed.\" [466] \"I salute the intelligence department of the royal court.\" [467] \"I'll give you fifteen hundred pounds.\" [468] Anthony looked at him in astonishment and then shook his head sadly. [469] \"I'm afraid it's not possible,\" he said regretfully. [470] \"Okay, then I'll give you two thousand pounds.\" [471] \"You're tempting me, Baron, it's very tempting, but I really can't do it.\" [472] \"How much do you want?\" [473] \"You don't understand what I mean. I believe you're a good man, and I understand that the memoir will ruin your plans. But I took on this task, and I have to complete it. You understand? I can't back out. I can't do that.\" [474] The baron listened attentively to what he said, then nodded several times. [475] \"I understand, it's your honor as a Britisher.\" [476] \"We won't be so formal,\" Anthony said. \"But, it's pretty much the same thing.\" [477] The Baron stood up. [478] \"I must respect the honor of a British gentleman,\" he said. \"We can only try other methods. Good morning.\" [479] His heels clicked together, and he bowed deeply, then stood upright and strode out of the room. [480] \"What does he mean?\" Anthony thought to himself. \"Threatening me? I'm not afraid of Lollipop. But, that name really suits him. I'll just call him Baron Lollipop from now on.\" [481] He paced around the room for a couple of circles, unsure of what to do next. It was October 5th, and there was still over a week until the deadline for the manuscript. Anthony figured he'd send it in on time, not before. Honestly, he was dying to see what the memoir was about. He had the same thought on the ship, but he was too feverish to concentrate on the messy handwriting, as the entire manuscript was handwritten. He had never wanted to see what was inside more than at that moment. [482] He had another task. [483] He quickly picked up the phonebook and searched for the surname \"Rueff,\" finding six \"Rueffs\": Edward Henry Rueff, a surgeon living on Harley Street; James Rueff & Company, a saddlery; Lennox Rueff, living in Abbottbury Manor, Hampstead City; Miss Mary Rueff, residing in Ealing; Mrs. Timothy Rueff, living at 487 Pound Street; and Mrs. Willie Rueff, living at 42 Grosvenor Square. The last four had a possibility of being the lady in question, but there was no evidence to suggest she lived in London. He shook his head lightly and closed the phonebook. [484] \"Now, we'll just have to see what happens,\" he said. \"No harm in trying.\" [485] People like Anthony Kede, who have good luck, often have it because they believe in luck. [486] Within half an hour, Anthony found what he was looking for while browsing through a magazine. He saw a portrait of some stage sets arranged by the Duchess of Pace. The central figure was a woman dressed in Eastern attire, and there was an inscription below her:\n[487] Mrs. Timothy Rue as Cleopatra. Miss Virginia Gaussland, daughter of Sir Edmundston, was formerly Miss Rue before her marriage. [488] Anthony stared at the picture for a while, then pursed his lips as if he were about to whistle. He tore the page out, folded it, and put it in his pocket. Then he went upstairs, opened his suitcase, and took out the package of letters. He took the folded magazine from his pocket and slipped it under the rope holding the package of letters. [489] Suddenly, he heard a sound behind him and turned around quickly. A man stood at the door with a face that Anthony thought only belonged to a character in a comedy musical troupe. He had a menacing appearance, a short and wide head, and a smile that showed his teeth. [490] \"What are you doing here?\" Anthony asked. \"Who let you up here?\" [491] \"No one can stop me,\" the stranger said, with a heavy voice that didn't sound like a native speaker but spoke fluent English. [492] \"Another foreigner,\" Anthony thought to himself. [493] \"Get out!\" he shouted. [494] The man stared hard at the letters in Anthony's hand. [495] \"Give me what I want, and I'll leave,\" he said. [496] \"What do you want?\" [497] The man stepped closer to him. [498] \"Stapleton's memoirs,\" he hissed. [499] \"You don't seem serious,\" Anthony said. \"You look like a villain from a stage play. I like your costume. Who sent you? Lord Lovelace?\" [500] \"Lovelace?\" The man uttered a series of broken and raspy sounds. [501] \"Do you always speak like that? The way you string words together sounds like a dog barking. I can't say it like you. Maybe our throats are constructed differently. I can only say Lord Ripley. So, was it him who sent you?\" [502] But Anthony's suggestion was met with a vehement rejection, and the unexpected visitor showed his disgust for the idea in a very direct manner. He reached into his pocket and threw a piece of paper onto the table. [503] \"Read it yourself,\" he said. \"After you read it, you'll be scared, you damn Englishman.\" [504] Anthony glanced at the paper out of curiosity, but did not fully comply with the man's second half of the instruction. The paper had a crude red imprint of a hand on it. [505] \"It looks like a hand,\" he said. \"But if you insist it's a stereoscopic image of a polar sunset, I wouldn't argue against that.\" [506] \"This is the symbol of the Red Hand Party, and I am a member of the Red Hand Party.\" [507] \"Really?\" Anthony looked at him curiously. \"So, are your comrades like you too? I wonder what the Eugenics Society would think if they saw you like this.\" [508] The man roared angrily. [509] \"Bastard,\" he said, \"a bastard even more despicable than a dog. Give me the memoirs, and you'll be fine. The Party is a brotherhood.\" [510] \"This Party is quite nice,\" Anthony said. \"But I'm afraid you and your comrades have misunderstood. I was instructed to send the manuscript to the publisher, not to your charming Party.\" [511] \"Piss!\" The other man laughed. \"Do you think you can make it to the publisher alive? Dream on. Give it to me, or I'll shoot.\" [512] He said, pulling a gun out of his pocket and waving it in the air. [513] Anthony Kede was not one to be trifled with. He didn't like people who acted before thinking. He wasn't going to just stand there and let a gun be pointed at him. Almost as soon as the other man pulled the gun, Anthony quickly stepped forward and knocked it out of his hand. The force of the blow made the man involuntarily turn around, facing away from Anthony. [514] Seizing the opportunity, Anthony quickly kicked his opponent with all his might, sending him flying from the doorway to the hallway and crashing to the ground. [515] Anthony followed him out. The tough Red Hand Party member quickly got up, rushed down the stairs. [516] Anthony didn't chase him, turning back to his own room. [517] \"The Red Hand is not as formidable as they seem. They look tough but are easily defeated. How did such a bunch of snakes and rats become a force? It's clear that this task is not as easy as I thought. Now, I'm against the royal family and the revolutionaries, and soon the nationalists and the independent freedomists will come. There's one thing I have to do urgently tonight: I have to start reading those manuscripts.\" [518] Anthony glanced at his watch and saw that it was almost nine o'clock, so he decided to have dinner in his room. Although he didn't want any unexpected guests, he thought he should be vigilant. He didn't want someone to steal his suitcase while he was downstairs in the restaurant. He called for the menu, ordered a few dishes and a bottle of Chablis red wine, and the waiter took the order and left. [519] While waiting for the food, he took out the manuscripts and put them on the table with the letters. [520] The doorbell rang, and the waiter entered the room with a small table and the order. Anthony walked over to the fireplace mantel, facing away from the room, standing in front of the mirror, aimlessly scanning his room, and then he noticed something strange. [521] The waiter's eyes were fixed on the stack of papers. [522] He moved around the table slowly, glancing at Anthony's unmoving back with the corner of his eye. His hands were trembling, and his tongue kept licking his dry lips. Anthony observed him more closely. He was tall, with no unusual features like the other waiters, his beard well-groomed, and his expression varied. Anthony thought he must be Italian, not French. [523] Suddenly, Anthony turned around, and the waiter was startled before quickly pretending to fiddle with the saltshaker. [524] \"What's your name?\" Anthony asked abruptly. [525] \"My name is Gis\u00e8ppe, sir.\" [526] \"Italian?\" [527] \"Yes.\" [528] Anthony spoke to him in Italian, and the server responded fluently. Anthony nodded and let him leave. As he enjoyed the food prepared by Gisippo, his mind raced. [529] Could he have misjudged? Was Gisippo's interest in the package merely out of ordinary curiosity? It was possible. But the thought of his intense excitement made Anthony dismiss the idea. He was still perplexed. [530] \"Damn it,\" Anthony thought to himself, \"how could everyone be after that blasted memoir? Must be my imagination.\" [531] After dinner and getting ready, he began to read the memoir. However, the old count's handwriting was too difficult to read, and the process progressed very slowly. The snail-like speed made him yawn repeatedly, and after reading the fourth chapter, he finally gave up. [532] So far, the memoir has been extremely boring, not even a hint of a scandal. [533] He gathered the letters and the packaging of the memoir on the table and locked them in his suitcase. Then he locked the door, placed a chair against it, and took a bottle of water from the bathroom and put it on the chair. [534] Anthony observed his defensive measures and then proudly took off his clothes and went to bed. He looked at the memoir again and soon felt his eyelids drooping. So he stuffed the manuscript under his pillow, turned off the lights, and quickly fell asleep. [535] About four hours later, Anthony was suddenly awakened by something. He didn't know what it was, it could be a sound, or just the danger awareness cultivated by his adventurous life for so many years. [536] He lay quietly for a while, trying to concentrate his attention. It was only when he heard the faint rustling sound that he realized there was a shadow squatting beside the suitcase next to the window. [537] Anthony jumped out of bed and quickly turned on the light. The figure squatting beside the suitcase stood up suddenly. It was the waiter, Gisippus. He held a long knife and rushed at Anthony. Anthony finally realized his dangerous situation. He was unarmed, while the other was well-prepared. He dodged to the side, and Gisippus missed. The two of them wrestled on the ground. Anthony tightly held Gisippus' right arm to push away his knife and twist his right arm behind him. But the Italian's left hand grabbed Anthony's throat, choking him. However, Anthony's hand holding the other's right arm did not let go. [538] \"Clang!\" The knife fell to the ground. The Italian turned sharply and broke free from Anthony's control. Anthony also got up and ran towards the door to block the other's way. But when he saw the intact chair and the water bottle, he suddenly realized he was mistaken, but it was too late. [539] Gisippus had entered the room through the window. While Anthony ran towards the door, he rushed to the balcony, jumped into the balcony of the next room, and escaped through the window of the next room. [540] Anthony knew he couldn't catch up with him. He had already planned his escape route, and pursuing him would only put himself in danger. [541] Anthony knew it was too late to chase him. He had planned his escape route and chasing him would only put himself in danger. He walked to the bedside, reached under the pillow, and took out the memoir. Luckily, he had not put it in the suitcase. He walked over to the suitcase and looked inside, wanting to take out the letters. [542] Then he cursed under his breath. [543] For he found that the letters were gone. [544] Chapter 6: Gentle Extortion\n[545] Virginia Rue returned to her residence on Pound Street at precisely 3:55 p.m., completely out of curiosity. She opened the door herself, but as soon as she stepped into the hallway, Chiffose called her without expression. [546] \"Excuse me, ma'am, someone wants to see you.\" [547] Chifley hesitated but Virginia didn't notice. [548] \"Mr. Roxburgh? Is he in the sitting room?\" [549] \"No, ma'am, not Mr. Roxburgh,\" Chifley said with a hint of regret in his voice. \"I didn't want to let him in, but he said he had something important to tell you about the late Major Rue. I thought you might want