d and then continued, \"You know, there's one thing that really surprises me. I had no idea that the memoir had such an unexpected mystery.\" [1523] \"You're right,\" Anthony agreed. \"There's one thing I want to ask you.\" [1524] \"What is it?\" [1525] \"When I mentioned Jimmy McGregor's name to you yesterday on Pound Street, why did you seem so surprised? Had you heard of him before?\" [1526] \"Sherlock Holmes, I have heard of him. George, my cousin George Roxborough, came to me one day and talked about a bunch of nonsense. He wanted me to come here and try to seduce this McGregor into giving up the memoir. Of course, he didn't say it like that. He talked about British ladies and all that useless stuff, but his intentions were clear. It's only poor old George who could come up with such a stupid idea. Later, I asked him a few more questions, and he started to lie to me. His lies were so bad that even a two-year-old child couldn't be fooled.\" [1527] \"Anyway, his plan seems to have succeeded,\" Anthony said. \"I came here, just as he wanted me to be Jimmy McGregor, and you were just what he wanted.\" [1528] \"But there's no memoir for poor old George! Now I have a question for you. When I said those letters weren't written by me, you said you knew. How did you know that?\" [1529] \"I do know,\" Anthony said with a smile. \"My psychological knowledge is quite solid.\" [1530] \"You mean you have confidence in my character, so...\"\n[1531] Anthony shook his head vigorously. [1532] \"Not at all. I know nothing about your character. You might have a lover and write him letters. But you wouldn't give in to blackmail. The real Virginia Ruff was terrified, but you held out.\" [1533] \"I'm curious about who the real 'Virginia Ruff' is, I mean where she is. It makes me feel like I have a doppelganger.\" [1534] Anthony lit a cigarette. [1535] \"Did you know that one of the letters was mailed from the Chimney House?\" he asked. [1536] \"What?\" Virginia was startled, \"When w