uldn't be surprised. Those tough guys are used to assassinating kings, and shooting a president just doesn't have the same thrill or challenge. As for kings, I just remembered something Steptoe said that night, that he knew the thugs who attacked him, they were from Viktor's court.\" [176] \"What?\" Anthony whirled around. [177] McGrath's mouth curved into a wider smile. [178] \"Getting excited, Mr. Gents?\" he drawled. [179] \"Be serious, Jimmy. This is quite important.\" [180] He walked over to the window and stood there, gazing out. [181] \"Who's Victor King?\" Jimmy asked. \"Another royal family from a Balkan country?\" [182] \"No,\" Anthony said slowly. \"He's not that kind of king.\" [183] \"Then who is he?\" [184] Anthony paused for a moment before saying, \"Jimmy, he's a con artist, a notorious jewel thief, and a brazen one at that. Victor King is just a codename he used in Paris. Paris is his gang's headquarters, and that's where he was caught. But they couldn't find enough evidence for his heavy crimes, so they gave him a light sentence of seven years. He's probably out by now, or maybe already has been.\" [185] \"Do you think Count Stapleton's imprisonment has anything to do with him? Were those thugs following him for revenge?\" [186] \"I don't know,\" Anthony said. \"It doesn't seem like it on the surface. As far as I know, Victor King hasn't touched the Hessian royal family. But it all looks so suspicious, doesn't it? Count Stapleton's death, that memoir, the rumors in the papers \u2013 everything is vague and intriguing. And I've also heard that Hessia has discovered oil. James, I have a strong feeling that they've been eyeing that insignificant little country.\" [187] \"Who?\" [188] \"The Hebrews, the yellow-faced financial moguls sitting in their offices.\" [189] \"So, what's your point?\" [190] \"Just making things complicated, that's all.\" [191] \"Not that,\" Anthony said, slightly disappointed. \"Guess where I'm planning to go with those two hundred and fifty pounds?\" [192] \"South America?\" [193] \"No, I'm going to Hesslovia. I think I could stand with that republic, and maybe even become its president.\" [194] \"Why don't you just claim to be a descendant of the Paulovich bloodline and go there to be a king?\" [195] \"No, James. A king has to reign for life, while a president only serves for four years. For me, a country like Hesslovia, four years is just right.\" [196] \"A king might not even last four years,\" Jimmy interjected. [197] \"I might not be able to resist taking a thousand pounds from your share. By the time you've brought back the gold, a thousand pounds won't even be a blip on your radar. So, I'll invest it in Hersovlav's oil mines. You know, James? This idea just keeps getting better and better. I never would have thought of Hersovlav if you hadn't mentioned it. I'll spend a day in London, grab the thousand pounds, and then catch the Balkan Express.\" [198] \"Don't rush off so quickly. I actually have something else I want you to do for me.\" [199] Anthony sat down on the chair, looking serious. [200] \"I always felt like there was something else, and sure enough, it's all coming together.\" [201] \"It's nothing like that. I just want you to do a small favor for a lady.\" [202] \"Not a chance. I'm not getting involved in your love affairs.\" [203] \"It's not like that. I haven't even met the woman. Let me tell you the whole story.\" [204] \"If you're going to ramble on, I'll have to have another drink.\" [205] The host hastened to oblige, and then began to relate the tale. [206] \"It happened in Uganda. I saved a South European man\u2026\"\n[207] \"James, if I were you, I'd write a book called 'The People I've Saved.' This is the second story I've heard tonight.\" [208] \"This time, I didn't do much. I just pulled him out of the river. It seems that most South Europeans can't swim.\" [209] \"Wait, is this related to something else?\" [210] \"No, not really. But I just remembered something strange. Although we call him Pedro, which is a Dutch name, he's actually from Hesse.\" [211] Anthony nodded indifferently, \"It doesn't matter what you call him, they're all South Europeans. Go on.\" [212] \"The guy might have been grateful, so he followed me like a dog. About six months later, he died of a fever. Before he passed away, he called me over and whispered a secret in my ear using some jargon, I thought he was talking about a gold mine, then he shoved a small oilcloth package into my hand. Well, I didn't take it seriously at the time. It wasn't until a week later that I opened the package. I admit, I was just too curious. I knew that with Pedro's intelligence, even if he saw a gold mine, he might not recognize it, but what if it was really a gold mine?\" [213] \"The thought of gold must have made your heart race, it's hard to resist,\" Anthony interrupted him. [214] \"I've never felt so disgusted in my life. Gold mine? Ha. For that scumbag, that thing could have been a gold mine. Do you know what it was? A letter from a woman, yes, a British woman. The bitch was blackmailing another woman, and she had the nerve to give me that filthy thing.\" [215] \"Your integrity is admirable, James, but South Europeans are South Europeans. His intentions were good. You saved his life, so he left his livelihood for you.\" [216] \"What am I supposed to do with this thing? Burn it? That's what I thought at first, but then I thought about that poor woman who wouldn't know the letters were destroyed and would be living in constant fear of that South European showing up again.\" [217] \"I never thought you had such an imaginative mind.\" Anthony said, lighting a cigarette, \"You're right, that makes it more complicated. Should we mail it to her?\" [218] \"Like most women, she didn't leave dates or addresses on most of the letters, but there was one with what looked like an address information, but only a name: Chimney Cottage.\" [219] Anthony was about to blow out the match in his hand when he suddenly froze, only to flick it away when the flame burned his fingers. [220] \"Chimney Cottage?\" he said. \"That's too bizarre.\" [221] \"Do you know it?\" [222] \"Darling, it's a British mansion, where the king and queen often spend weekends, and diplomats gather there.\" [223] \"I'd rather you go to England than me, because you know all about these things. I'm just a Canadian country bumpkin who would make a mess of things. You're worldly, you've been to Eton and Harrow\u2026\"\n[224] \"Only Eton.\" Anthony said modestly. [225] \"Someone like you could handle this. You were asking me why I didn't just mail them to her? I thought it was too dangerous. I suspect her husband is jealous. What if he accidentally opened them? Or maybe the woman is dead, because the letters seem quite old. I think the only proper thing to do is to take those letters to England and hand them to her personally.\" [226] Anthony dropped his cigarette and walked over to his friend, patting him affectionately on the back. [227] \"You're a real knight in shining armor, Jimmy,\" he said. \"Your little Canadian town should be proud of you. If I were in your shoes, I wouldn't do half as well.\" [228] \"So you're agreeing?\" [229] \"Of course.\" [230] McGregor stood up, walked over to the drawer, took out a packet of letters, and dropped them on the table. [231] \"Here, you better take a look at these first.\" [232] \"Do I really need to?\" [233] \"Based on what you told me about the chimney house, she probably just stayed there for a while. We should go through these letters and see if we can find any clues to figure out where she really lives.\" [234] \"I agree with you.\" [235] They carefully went through all the letters, but found nothing. Anthony thoughtfully gathered the letters together. [236] \"Poor thing,\" he said, \"she must have been terrified.\" [237] Jimmy nodded. [238] \"Do you think you can find her?\" he asked, worriedly. [239] \"I'll stay in Britain if I can't find her. You seem to care a lot about this stranger, James.\" [240] Jimmy absent-mindedly stroked the signature on the letter. [241] \"Her name is lovely,\" he explained. \"Her name is Virginia Ruff.\" [242] * * *\n[243] [1] A ducat, a currency used in Europe during the Middle Ages. [244] Chapter Three: The Troubles of the Upper Class\n[245] \"Indeed, my friend, indeed,\" the Earl of Carrington said, dismissively. [246] The Earl of Carrington, Edward Alastair Brent, was the ninth Earl of Carrington. He was short and shabbily dressed, looking nothing like a nobleman. His eyes were pale blue, and his long, narrow nose gave him a somewhat melancholy appearance. His manners, while somewhat stiff, were impeccable. [247] For the ninth Earl of Carrington, the greatest misfortune of his life was inheriting his brother's title four years ago. The previous Earl had been a household name in England, a prominent figure in the government, and a key player in the British Parliament. His country estate, the Smokestack Villa, was renowned for its hospitality. His wife, the daughter of the Duke of Posh, had played a big part in that. There were always informal gatherings at the Smokestack Villa on weekends, where glasses were raised and deals were made and broken. The who's who of British and European aristocracy had all spent time there. [248] All of that was well and good, but the ninth Earl of Carrington couldn't help but feel a sense of awe when he thought of his brother, Henry, who had done all of that. The ninth Earl simply disagreed with the idea of Smokestack Villa being considered a national asset rather than a private country house. He hated it when non-politicians talked politics, which is why George Romax's incessant prattle was so irritating. Romax was a robust man, slightly overweight, with a ruddy complexion and bulging eyes that exuded an air of self-importance. [249] \"Do you understand what I mean, Carrington? We simply cannot afford any scandal at this point. The situation is delicate,\" Romax said. [250] \"You understand my meaning, Carrington? We cannot afford any scandal at this point. The situation is delicate.\" [251] \"Always has been.\" Lord Carrington replied with a touch of sarcasm. [252] \"I'm a man of action, Carrington.\" [253] \"Indeed, indeed.\" Lord Carrington reverted to his previous tactic. [254] \"If there is any mistake in Hesslovakia, we are done for. The most important thing is to grant the oil drilling rights to a British company. Please pay attention, will you?\" [255] \"Of course, of course.\" [256] \"Prince Michael O'Paulwich arrives this weekend, organizing a hunting party at the Chimney House to provide a cover, and the whole thing will be wrapped up.\" [257] \"I was planning to go abroad this week, actually,\" said the Earl of Carringham. [258] \"Nonsense, my dear Carringham! Who goes abroad in the first week of October?\" [259] \"My doctor thinks my health is very poor,\" said the Earl of Carringham, gazing longingly at the taxi passing by. [260] However, he could not escape, as Roxemus always clung to anyone he spoke to about serious matters, and this annoying habit of his had been going on for some time. In this particular situation, he was holding tightly onto the Earl of Carringham's collar. [261] \"Brother, I solemnly entrust this to you. It's a time of national crisis, and what's coming\u2026\"\n[262] The Earl of Carrington fidgeted uncomfortably. He suddenly felt that he would rather organize a hundred more parties than listen to Lord Roxane rambling on with his own speech for twenty more minutes. Based on experience, he knew that Lord Roxane would continue to speak for at least another twenty minutes. [263] \"All right,\" he said quickly, \"I'll take care of it, so you arrange everything.\" [264] \"There's nothing to arrange. The Chimney House is the perfect place, and it has so much history. I'll go to the convent, which is only about seven miles away. I won't attend the banquet, as it's not appropriate.\" [265] \"Yes, it's not appropriate,\" agreed the Earl of Carrington, although he didn't know why it wasn't appropriate, and he didn't care to find out. [266] \"However, we should invite Bill Eversley. He can help pass on the message.\" [267] \"That would be splendid,\" the Earl of Carringham perked up a bit, \"Bill is a good shot, and Bunder likes him.\" [268] \"Shooting isn't important; it's just a cover.\" [269] The Earl of Carringham's spirits sank again, and he looked dejected. [270] \"That's all right. The King, the King's retinue, Bill Eversley, Herman Eisencastle...\n[271] \"Who?\" [272] \"Herman Eisencamp. The representative from the consortium I mentioned.\" [273] \"The British consortium?\" [274] \"Yes, what about it?\" [275] \"Nothing, nothing. I was just curious. The name is quite unusual.\" [276] \"By the way, we still need one or two outsiders to make it look legit. Miss Eileen will certainly notice that. We need to find young, non-gossipy, and politically clueless ones.\" [277] \"Bonder will notice that.\" [278] \"I just thought of something.\" Roxas suddenly looked like he remembered something, \"Do you remember what I said earlier?\" [279] \"You said a lot of things.\" [280] \"I mean the unfortunate incident.\" He lowered his voice and whispered into the ear of the Marquis, \"Staunton's memoirs.\" [281] \"I think you're overreacting.\" Sir Henry restrained himself from yawning, \"People like scandals. I myself also read some memoirs and found them very interesting.\" [282] \"It's not whether people read or don't read, they will all rush to read it, and that's the problem. At this critical moment, publishing such a book will ruin everything, everything! The people of Hesse-Slavonia hope for the restoration. Prince Michael has the support and encouragement of His Majesty the King, and the people of Hesse-Slavonia are also prepared to acclaim him.\" [283] \"Who is the one who is preparing to grant the oil concession to the Herman Eisakstein Company to obtain a million loans for succession?\" [284] \"Carterham, Carterham,\" Roamax begged in a painful tone in his ear, \"be careful, you must be careful.\" [285] \"So the question is... \" The Marquis lowered his voice and continued with interest, \"The events mentioned in Staunton's memoirs will make their dreams come true, mainly the atrocities and vices of the House of Paulovich, right? Then the question will be raised in the parliament: why change the current liberal democracy to a backward autocratic rule? Just because of the policies of the bloodsucking capitalists? Then there will be calls for 'down with the government'... Is that right?\" [286] Roxane nodded.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[2374] \"Kid, he got up early. He's staying, and I'm fine with that. Although I don't know him well, I like him a lot, really like him.\" [2375] \"Virginia too,\" Bendl said, drawing a pattern on the table with his fork. [2376] \"Oh?\" [2377] \"I do too, but it seems pointless.\" [2378] \"I also invited Azerbaijan.\" The Earl of Carringham continued. [2379] \"And?\" [2380] \"Luckily, he's going back to London. By the way, don't forget to have them arrange for a car to take him to the train station for the 10:50 train.\" [2381] \"Okay.\" [2382] \"Now, I just need to get rid of Fitch,\" the Earl of Carrington said, perking up. [2383] \"I thought you enjoyed talking about your moldy old books with him.\" [2384] \"I do, I do. Well, I did a few days ago. But when you realize you're the only one talking, it can get quite boring. Fitch is interested, but he never offers an opinion.\" [2385] \"That's better than always listening to someone else talk,\" Bandel said, \"like George Romax.\" [2386] The Earl of Chatham shuddered at the thought. [2387] \"George is good at speeches on the stage,\" said Bunder, \"even though I know he's just spouting nonsense. I've still clapped for him. Anyway, I'm a socialist.\" [2388] \"I know, dear, I know,\" said the Earl of Chatham hastily. [2389] \"Well, let's not discuss politics at home anymore,\" said Bunder. \"Public speeches are more George's thing. Parliament should pass a bill to outlaw that habit.\" [2390] \"Right,\" said the Earl of Chatham. [2391] \"And Virginia?\" Bunder asked. \"Did you invite her to stay?\" [2392] \"Bartle said he was inviting everyone.\" [2393] \"He said it with conviction! Have you told her about being my stepmother?\" [2394] \"I don't think it would have made a difference,\" the Marquess of Carrham said sadly. \"Although she called me dear last night. But that's the worst thing about gentle young women. They can say anything, but saying it doesn't mean anything.\" [2395] \"That's true,\" Bunder agreed. \"If she kicked you or bit you, she might have a better chance.\" [2396] \"You young people today don't know how to have a proper relationship,\" the Earl of Carrington lamented. [2397] \"We learned it all from 'Chief',\" Bendl said, \"abandoning love, playing hard to get, and so on.\" [2398] \"What is 'Chief'?\" the Earl of Carrington asked briefly, \"Is it a poem?\" [2399] Bendl looked at him pityingly and then stood up, kissing the top of his head. [2400] \"Dear father,\" she said, and then lightly walked out of the window. [2401] The Marquess of Carringham resumed his study of the news from the rare book dealer. [2402] After a while, Mr. Hale Fiskle surprised the Marquess of Carringham by greeting him. He always did this, walking silently. [2403] \"Good morning, Marquess of Carringham,\" he said. [2404] \"Good morning,\" said the Marquess of Carringham, \"Good morning, it's a fine day today.\" [2405] \"Indeed, it is,\" said Mr. Fiskle. [2406] He poured himself a cup of coffee and took a slice of white toast for breakfast. [2407] \"I heard the ban has been lifted, is that true?\